At  9:48 AM 9/3/93 -0400, Jim Demmers wrote:
>X-Message-Number: #93-9-71
>>-- Ray Cromwell:
>>  Our "claim" on the biosphere is nothing more than our ability to take
>>advantage of it. Why humans must be held to a higher standard I do not
>>know. The beaver does not consider the consequences of the dam he builds
>>anymore than the wolf cares about lamb population.
>
>Ah...but is the beaver sentient, and is he aware of the greater environment
>that surrounds him as we are.  I think THAT awareness, coupled with the act 
>of taking responsibility for one's own actions, is key in determining the 
>standard by which our actions should be measured.

Why does that affect us?  Yes, taking responsibility for one's actions is
important... but why should we have responsibility toward entities that
aren't capable of recognizing responsibility in return or entering into
contracts?  My own environmentalism (which is growing increasingly slight
as I read great stuff like Bailey's "Eco-Scam" and Julian Simon's article
in the new Extropy) is based solely on human valuation:

We (humankind) are likely to need this ecosystem to sustain human culture
for at least another thirty or forty years, and probably for a few hundred
or thousand if events unfold more slowly than we hope.  We know we don't
understand it yet, so we should refrain from large-scale mucking about with
it.  One doesn't open the air plant in a spacecraft and start diddling it
with a screwdriver if one depends on it for breathing-air and it seems to
be working okay.

Our responsibility is only to ourselves, to not fuck up the ecology such
that it can no longer sustain us.  Since beavers (e.g.) might perform some
important function in the ability of the Earth to support human life, we
probably shouldn't wipe them all out until we know for sure that they
don't.  Pretending that we owe them something, that they are agents and
have some kind of rights just because they exist, is silly.  (NOTE:  I am
not a "natural law" believer; I base my moral system on aesthetics.  I
don't believe that humans have any "rights" either.  They do, however, have
the capability to enter into contracts with me giving us each "rights" with
respect to each other, and so I am willing to do that to our mutual
benefit.)

>I make no "claim" to
>the biosphere other than my right to inhabit the space in which I find
>myself at any given moment.

Far out.  I think you may want to revise that statement.

Do you leave the doors of your house open when you go to work?  After all,
you're not there at the moment, and it might rain and some other biosphere
participants (squirrels, stray cats, whatever) might need the space to stay
dry.  Do you drive an automobile or purchase manufactured products?  Then
you are impacting the environment in distant places in ways of which you
have no detailed knowledge.

I am not claiming there is anything wrong with this; quite the contrary.  I
just think you should know you're doing it.  I always get amused by
"back-to-nature" types who plan to turn their back on evil industrial
culture, and then buy steel shovels and trowels at the local Hardware Hank.

Your limited claim on the biosphere doesn't seem to include any room to
procreate, either.

I define my own relationship to the biosphere in terms of my desire for my
life and my culture to continue to exist and grow.  If the biosphere is
necessary for those things to take place (or, being risk-averse, I suspect
it _might_ be necessary), then I will try to refrain from wrecking it.  If
the biosphere is instead in the way, well, tough luck spotted owl.

>If I find that I must intrude into the space of
>another, I make a reasonable attempt at accomodation (reasonable being a
>relative term).  Being at the top of the food chain, it behooves us to pay
>attention to the ripple effect our actions have on the lower echelons.
>- jim

To be sure.  But only because those ripples might come back to affect our
own survival or comfort.  Otherwise, fuck 'em if they can't take a joke.

You, on the other hand, seem to indicate that other organisms have "rights"
independent of their utility for humans.  I find that interesting and would
like to know why you think this (if in fact you do).
