Date: Fri, 3 Sep 93 11:11:18 PDT
From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: WAR/NANO/LAW, "nanarchy" and crime

I welcome Eric Drexler in joining this conversation.  We'll have to
step carefully, though, as it is not clear that Mark Miller and Eric
Drexler are worried about the same problems, or proposing the same
solution.  

Eric doesn't like Mark's name "nanarchy" (but suggests no substitute).
Rather than a sea of nano-Gorts preventing all coercion, Eric proposes
"stable, automated security arrangements".  And instead of seeing
interstellar colonization as the cause of a single power, Eric looks
to "a rather abrupt transition to a world with molecular manufacturing
and machine intelligence".

Since they both are concerned about future crime, and since Eric
challenges me to describe alternatives, I'll now describe what I
envision.  (I'll address other issues in another post.)  Again I'm not
saying this approach is optimal, just workable, and thus
"post-enforcement" can remain workable in the future.  Pre-enforcement
is not our only conceivable option.

I imagine that before any entity (human, upload, AI, itsy bit of
software or nanotech) is allowed to enter a "region", so that it
obtains a substantially increased ability to cause harm in that
region, it must demonstrate some ability to pay should it cause harm
there.

Some conservative estimate would be made of what any entity might
think it had to gain by causing harm, of the amount of harm it might
thereby cause, of the chance that that entity could be identified as
the culprit if it did so cause harm, and of the cost of such
investigation.  (Private speculators can estimate some of this.)  The
entity would have to then show it had enough wealth or crime insurance
to cover all this, or enough wealthy ancestors and siblings to "vouch"
for it.

With humans, one might also accept conservative estimates of how much
they value their own life.  The region would then have good reason to
expect that they could either deter crime by threating to punish
criminals by more than those criminals could expect to benefit from
the crime, or they could expect to be repaid for at least the damage
of a crime.

Of course more "humane" regions may decide they had other ways to
determine the "good intentions" of their entities, and so relax the
above requirements.  Or they might just rely heavily on history,
assuming that some form of crime prevention is sufficient until they
see some new form of AI get around it.  My point again is not to say
this is optimal, just workable.

So regarding Eric's questions, parent and sibling relationships need
not be traced with certainty.  Those entities that decline to make
such tracing easy would pay for the consequences of this some other
way.  Punishment can be by payment of fines.  And regarding Mark
Grant's question, I envisioned the fine being taken out of the hide of
the entity directly responsible, and if selling their carcus isn't
enough to pay it, then they go to parents and siblings.

Robin Hanson

