Nick Szabo writes:
> Mark Grant:
> > [programmed AI vs. acting on its own]
> > This kind of programming 
> > would be easy for an algorithmic organism, but also easily spotted, 
> 
> I'm not so sure about this.  The programmer can use encryption and
> self-destruction ("digital flash paper") to hide the algorithm.  
> The AI might in other ways pass a "free choice Turing test" but still
> have this assasin module built in.

I was thinking more of code-analysis than a Turing test. For a neural network
or something like it, that's impossible, but for the sort of AI that you could
program to carry out an action with 100% certainty, you should be able to
find that programming in the code, *if you can get at the code*.

Yes, I agree that there are plenty of ways of hiding the incriminating 
instructions, but any AI that includes code to copy bits of itself into
data space and modify them, or to decrypt bits of itself and execute them, or 
to delete parts of itself, should be considered strong circumstantial evidence 
against the "parent".

> There's also the whole question of *intent*: did the AI intend
> to kill the victim, or was it accidental, in which case (a)
> could the AI have been reasonably expected to be programmed to
> avoid the situation, or (b) could the AI have been reasonably 
> expected to make the free choice to avoid the situation.

Good points, but if we're allowing the possibility of AIs being dragged into 
court in some sense, then it's for the courts to decide in each case and share 
out the blame. Maybe we would have to add 'innocent due to bad programming' to 
the standard innocent and guilty verdicts. (Perhaps with an implied 'negligent 
programmer' attached ?)

> > *joint* responsibility, and/or compulsory termination of the AI ?
> 
> This might be a good idea.  Pets or wild animals that kill people are
> typically destroyed, and their owners sometimes found negligent.
> 
> Nick Szabo					szabo@netcom.com
> 

This is another good example from current law. I think that one of the major
'legal' issues in the next century or two is going to be decisions on these ideas 
of intelligence and responsibility (both for AIs and 'animals', especially including
the sorts of engineered human/animal crossbreeds that we might expect within a 
century or two).

Currently, a 'stupid' 'electronic child' (like a PC virus) is considered in many
countries to pass responsibility to the human parent, and I suspect that in a 
similar way a more intelligent animal trained to kill someone when told to "kill" 
would, if it killed someone on command, pass responsiblity for that death to the 
person who told it to "kill", even if it was "terminated" itself. On the other hand, 
an AI which was regarded to be as intelligent as a human should assume as much 
responsibility as a human. It all seems to come down to a definition of 
"intelligence" or "sapience", or perhaps "responsibility".

Basically, I think that the lawyers have had it easy for the last few centuries, 
being able to say 'Oh that's a human, they're responsible' or 'Oh, that's an animal, 
they aren't responsible'. The next few centuries look to require an awful lot more
thought on these categories (e.g. 'Oh, that's an AI based on Gnu-AI v2.3.17 code,
that's responsible', or 'Oh, that's a centaur, that's responsible' or 'That's a
DOS-based virus, that's not responsible'). 

One view of this in fiction that I read a long time back was in "Little Fuzzy" (or
similar) by H. Beam Piper, where certain humans wanted to kill off an animal on an
alien planet to get at the resources in the area these animals lived in, and other 
people wanted to show them to be sentient so that killing them would count as murder. 
I don't remember the particular definition of sentience they used, but I'm sure some 
such legal definition will be needed.


						Mark


