Date: Fri, 3 Sep 93 13:44:29 PDT
From: thamilto@pcocd2.intel.com (Tony Hamilton - FES ERG~)
Subject: Green Goo

(I tend to write too much, so I categorized my thoughts in case you'd rather
 not read the speculative parts...)

INTRODUCTION

Nick wrote me in email, wondering how I could imagine an ever-increasing
population on Earth, given Green Goo. I had not read the article, so I
thank Nick for re-sending it to me in email.

I have, I believe, a simple question to pose. First, however, I would like
to point out that I do not dispute the potential for Green Goo (neither do
I support a claim that it is inevitable). 

QUESTION

My question is, how would Green Goo be unlike the nuclear threat? Again,
I am not disputing either threat, but just curious to know how either is
tactically different from the other. We have, for quite some time now,
had large powers with the ability to destroy virtually all humanity (and,
in fact, all humanity if you perhaps believe in Sagan's nuclear winter, 
which would be many times worse with a complete launch of all weapons. I
don't buy into the entire nuclear winter theory, but it's there anyway).
And, there have been for a while now, a number of smaller powers with the
ability to develop and deliver at least a limited amount of mass destruction
to certain areas of the world.

Now, soon, even smaller factions yet will hold the nuclear button in their
hands, and that may be a very real threat, one which I understand is now
the United State's focus with respect to its nuclear arsenal (as opposed
to cold war objective of deterring nuclear attack from the Soviet Union).

PONDERING

So, isn't Green Goo just a member of a larger class of threats,
nuclear destruction being one, which have been around, and will potentially
increase in number, in the years to come? Can we say with absolute
certainty that there exists the possibility for a Green Goo to be engineered
which can be delivered across the entire planet? I can certainly see entire
countries or continents succumbing to such a threat, but again, it brings
us back into the category of any of a number of other potential threats of
similar magnitude.

SPECULATION

Now, personally, I don't believe there are black and white absolutes when
it comes to nearly anything, including viruses. If Green Goo is released,
then a couple things will happen. First, people will die. Next, a cure might
be found, but if not in time, then people will simply leave the planet.
As a worst case, this could seriously diminish the human population in the
short term. But in the long term, a way will be figured out to safely return
(assuming no counter was found before all humans who could had to leave).

PREDICTION

How do you keep it from happening again? Well, once you can at least ensure
that the human race isn't _entirely_ wiped out (humans living somewhere
else in the solar system - space, moon, mars, whatever), then the whole
Green Goo issue simply becomes a market like anything else. Businesses which
make the stuff will exist, and business which counter the stuff will. On
any given planet, or at any given colony, the cycle continues until and 
unless that entire planet or colony is bought out by a single person or
company or other such entity. That entity can then more effectively deal
with external factors which would threaten it with Green Goo or other
catastrophe.

So, perhaps there will be a "Goo period" in the future, where this kind
of thing boils down to market economics like anything else. Eventually, I
think you'd find that the ones causing the problems would end up defeating
themselves, and the goo/green/gaia/whatever memes would not survive.

CONCLUSION

Am I rationalizing? Of course, but I think it is a useful excercise, because
if you're just going to take the stance that Green Goo will happen, and that
there will be no possible reaction, then what is the point anyway? What
options does this present us with? Working towards a better understanding
of microbiology, genetics, physics, and so forth, so as to stay a step
ahead of "The Goo", or demonstrating a real capacity to get into space and
stay there. Since I don't believe Green Goo is the only possible threat, I
think the latter pursuit may be of a higher priority, although the former
is also important.

